---
import FoundationPage from "../components/FoundationPage.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import UsagePreview from "../components/UsagePreview.astro";
import { installUsageTree } from "../demos/installation";
import { getLocale, useTranslations } from "../i18n";
import { installCommand, kitVersion } from "../lib/install-command";

const installVanilla = installCommand("vanilla");
const installReact = installCommand("react");

const imports = `/* Base: palettes, scale, semantics and state layer. */
@import "@skryensya/vanilla/css/tokens.css";

/* Opt-in: Import only the components you ship. */
@import "@skryensya/vanilla/css/components/button.css";`;

const t = useTranslations(getLocale(Astro.url));
---

<FoundationPage
  title="Installation"
  description="Sass tokens and components: shared core, vanilla progressive enhancement and React."
  heading="skryensya/ui"
>
  <p class="lede">
    Sass tokens that compile to CSS runtime and components. Tokens still use custom properties, cascade layers and native functions; Components add behavior when the platform does not provide it. If those terms do not sound familiar to you, start with the <a href="/prerequisites">prerequisites</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <p>
    The packages are not on npm yet: that starts with v0.1.0. Until then each one installs from the
    tarball on its GitHub Release ({kitVersion} here). A tarball names the Skryensya packages it depends
    on by version, which npm cannot resolve, so the command lists them too.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={installVanilla} label="Vanilla" />
  <CodeBlock code={installReact} label="React" />
  <p>
    The binding re-exports the core's stylesheets under <code>css/</code>, so you never import
    <code>@skryensya/core</code> yourself. To compile the tokens with your own Sass instead, import
    <code>tokens.scss</code> from <code>@skryensya/core</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Importing</h2>
  <p>
    Naked specifiers are resolved by the package's <code>exports</code> map, so you need a bundler (Vite, webpack, Parcel). This site is built exactly like this.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={imports} label="CSS" />

  <h2>Use</h2>
  <p>
    For static components, the class is enough: the CSS brings the structure and its styling hooks. For machined components, <code>@skryensya/vanilla</code> hydrates the authored HTML and <code>@skryensya/react</code> renders the same contract.
  </p>

  <UsagePreview tree={installUsageTree(t)} label="Button" />

  <h2>Components with behavior</h2>
  <p>
    Tabs is already mounted on site as a full vertical slice: <a href="/components/tabs">see component</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Try it live</h2>
  <p>
    The controls above change the mode, color palette and contrast. Ramps are configured once from <code>:root</code>, without combinations or brand selectors on disk. Change them and look at the buttons. Then look at the <a class="sk-link sk-interactive" href="/reference">token reference</a>, where each resolved value is read back live.
  </p>
</FoundationPage>
